--- a/net/knet/docs/design.md
+++ b/net/knet/docs/design.md
@@ -285,6 +285,15 @@
 
   A blocking Unix stream `connect` waits for listener backlog capacity through `event-listener` when the backlog is full. The wait is bounded by `SO_SNDTIMEO` and therefore requires scheduler support and an interruptible task context.
 
+  `GeneralOptions::send_poller_with_nonblocking` and its receive counterpart
+  check the socket's own nonblocking flag and the per-call override first: when
+  either is set they run the operation once and return, bypassing the timeout
+  wrapper and the readiness wait. A nonblocking call must report progress,
+  `WouldBlock`, or a real error; it must not be converted into `EINTR` by a
+  signal that happens to be pending when the call is issued. Only blocking
+  operations enter the interruptible wait, and callers rely on that boundary —
+  a window manager that reopens its menus is defeated by spurious `EINTR`.
+
 * **Global state is not tied to a fixed current process or thread**
 
   Shared access to `SERVICE`, `SOCKET_SET`, and Router state is mainly protected by global locks and atomic state. Syscall-specific semantics are still provided by `posix/net`, which supplies the process file-descriptor context and caller credentials.
@@ -851,6 +860,46 @@
 
 Readable, writable, and connection-state events use separate `PollSet` instances for each endpoint. A write wakes the peer's readable waiters. A read wakes the peer's writable waiters when buffer occupancy crosses the send-buffer low-water mark. A half-close wakes the affected direction, and a full close notifies both endpoints through their connection-state waiters.
 
+### Unix Stream Control Messages
+
+A Unix stream carries ancillary data (`SCM_RIGHTS`, `SCM_CREDENTIALS`) in a side
+queue attached to the in-band bytes, because descriptors passed over `sendmsg`
+must stay associated with the message they were sent with. Each transmit
+direction owns a `StreamControl` (`unix/stream/channel.rs`) holding wrapping
+byte positions `written`/`read` and a FIFO of `ControlRecord`. The transmit
+control queue of one endpoint is the receive control queue of its peer, so
+`Channel` holds both `tx_control` and `rx_control` references to the same pair
+of `Arc<Mutex<StreamControl>>`.
+
+`send` publishes one record for the first batch that actually writes bytes:
+
+1. read into the vacant ring region and compute `count`;
+2. lock `tx_control`, and reserve capacity when a record will be published, so
+   publishing under `tx_order` neither allocates nor drops descriptors;
+3. under `tx_order`, recheck shutdown state, push the record covering
+   `[written, written + count)`, advance `written`, and publish the write index.
+
+Later batches of the same `send` carry no record, so a partially accepted
+message never delivers its descriptors twice.
+
+`recv` treats the front record as a receive barrier:
+
+- the read is limited to `end - read` bytes of that record (bounded by the
+  occupied length and the caller's buffer), so one `recvmsg` cannot merge two
+  rights lists;
+- a record is delivered once the read crosses the position of its first byte,
+  which the limit above makes an all-or-nothing condition;
+- `MSG_PEEK` clones the record instead of removing it, so a peek does not
+  consume the descriptors and a later read still receives them;
+- the guard is dropped, the peer endpoint reference is cloned, and the channel
+  mutex is released **before** the received descriptors are appended to the
+  caller's list, because dropping an unclaimed rights list can close a socket
+  and reenter this transport.
+
+`AncillaryData` is `Arc<dyn Any + Send + Sync>`, not `Box`: sharing is what
+lets a peek and the syscall layer both observe a queued resource. Transports
+that discard a record (`Channel::drop`) release it outside every state lock.
+
 ### Packet and vsock Synchronization
 
 `PACKET_HANDLERS` protects weak socket registrations with a mutex and tracks the active count atomically. Each packet socket uses an `RwLock` for its binding, a mutex for its bounded RX queue, and a separate `recv_lock` to serialize receive/peek operations across payload copying. Packet and drop statistics use atomic counters. Frame publication snapshots active sockets before accessing their queues.
@@ -988,6 +1037,31 @@
 Data flows from TIPC to vsock through `get_msg -> read_msg -> put_msg -> send_bridge`. When vsock credits are exhausted, `send_bridge` blocks on `tx_wait_queue` until `CreditUpdate`. Connection closure returns an error for the caller to handle. Bridge v1 forwards only bytes; TIPC handles and memory references are outside its scope.
 
 With `vsock_tipc_bridge` enabled, `bind()` rejects explicit binding to ports in `BRIDGE_PORT_MAP`, and ephemeral port allocation skips these reserved ports.
+
+### TCP Keep-Alive Tuning
+
+Linux exposes keep-alive as `SO_KEEPALIVE` plus three independent knobs
+(`TCP_KEEPIDLE`, `TCP_KEEPINTVL`, `TCP_KEEPCNT`) whose defaults live in
+`net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_{time,intvl,probes}`. smoltcp has one knob: the interval
+after which an idle connection is probed. The three options are therefore split
+by what the stack can actually honour.
+
+| Option | Effect |
+|---|---|
+| `TCP_KEEPIDLE` | Recorded, and applied to smoltcp's keep-alive interval when keep-alive is already enabled. The two quantities are the same one: how long the connection must be idle before the first probe. |
+| `TCP_KEEPINTVL` | Recorded and reported back by `getsockopt`; smoltcp probes at the single interval above, so there is no second knob to apply it to. |
+| `TCP_KEEPCNT` | Recorded and reported back; smoltcp decides on its own when to give up. |
+
+Setting any of the three does not by itself enable probing; only
+`SO_KEEPALIVE` does, which is also Linux's behaviour. `SO_KEEPALIVE` uses the
+currently configured idle time, so the two orders of `SO_KEEPALIVE` and
+`TCP_KEEPIDLE` agree.
+
+Accepting values the stack cannot yet enforce is a deliberate trade. The
+alternative -- `ENOPROTOOPT` -- is not neutral in practice: Chromium's network
+service treats it as a failed connection setup and retries, which was observable
+here as an uninterrupted `sockopt rejected: (6, 4)` stream on the kernel console
+that flooded the serial link and made guest-side evidence collection unreliable.
 
 ## Supported Scope and Limitations
 
@@ -1000,7 +1074,7 @@
 | AF_PACKET | Ethernet raw/datagram sockets with byte- and frame-bounded queues; filters `PACKET_OTHERHOST`. Membership changes, connect, peer queries, shutdown, listen, and accept are unsupported. |
 | rtnetlink operations | Supports link queries/updates, IPv4 address and route queries/add/delete, and IPv4 neighbor updates. Other message types return `NLMSG_ERROR` with `EOPNOTSUPP`; neighbors accept only incomplete/permanent states with no flags. |
 | Netlink sockets | Route sockets reject multicast subscriptions, userspace-peer destinations, and mixed query/mutation batches. Kobject-uevent sockets support group delivery, `SO_PASSCRED`, and validated no-op classic-BPF attach/detach compatibility. Connect, peer queries, and shutdown are unsupported. |
-| Socket options | Options unhandled by the selected protocol return `ENOPROTOOPT`. |
+| Socket options | Options unhandled by the selected protocol return `ENOPROTOOPT`. TCP keep-alive tuning is accepted and readable, but only `TCP_KEEPIDLE` reaches the stack; `TCP_KEEPINTVL` and `TCP_KEEPCNT` are stored for `getsockopt` and not yet enforced. |
 | Unix datagrams | Receive queues use unbounded `async_channel` queues. |
 | vsock / TIPC bridge | Stream transport only; the bridge forwards bytes, excluding TIPC handles and memory references. |
 | Ethernet progress | Failed RX-scheduler attachment falls back to timeout polling. ARP pending traffic is subject to head-of-line blocking. |
